uld be  believed or trusted now this applies in  more literally chosen characters as  opposed to characters chosen by default  so this is something that actually  bugged me in the end of time that's the  Doctor Who two-parter that ended David  Tennant's initial run as the doctor and  that is that the time Lords are  operating on the basis of a prophecy now  the time Lords depending on when you are  watching Doctor Who either are very  mysterious or are surprisingly  pedestrian but this whole idea of this  more mystical aspect is not something  that's really seriously rooted in most  of the Canon material by Canon I mean  the TV show the various things like  novels comics and whatever are basically  Canon until they're not because the show  is not obligated aided to hold true to  them so part of why that really bothered  me was that since mysticism of that kind  wasn't really firmly established as  being part of Time Lord culture again  not in the show and certainly not in the  revived version of the show up to that  point the idea of just injecting a  prophecy my first question is  why why do you believe this falling all  of it falling  black and pitch and screaming fire so  burning why are you assuming the  correctness of that and most stories  don't bother to establish that now  sometimes it doesn't matter sometimes  all that matters is that certain  characters believe it now this usually  works better if it is the villain who  believes it so to come back to say The  Dark Crystal which uh if you hadn't  guessed is an example I am leaning on  rather heavily to avoid having to bring  up Harry Potter because otherwise that  would be a one that I could cite readily  in this but I don't want to  um but in the Dark Crystal again the  thing that matters is less that the  prophecy is absolutely true that all the  that a gelfling will end them then the  fact that the skeksis believed that  enough  to wipe out the gelflings or at least  try to  so that is a situation where 